  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 400 AM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nSouthwesterly to near zonal flow is found over the northern \nPlains this morning. To south of the forecast area a upper level\nlow linger stalls over the central Great Plains, contributing \nto a slight low level moisture advection up north across our \narea. This ongoing moisture advection has contributed to \nwidespread low level stratus developments over much of central \nand portions of western North Dakota, though cloud cover over \nthe far western portions of the state remains light. Where clear\nskies remain areas of radiation fog, occasionally dense have \ndeveloped, and are expected to linger through the mid to late \nmorning. Both fog and stratus are generally expected to diminish\nthrough the late morning into the early afternoon, though \ninterrogation of BUFKIT model soundings across the southern \nJames River Valley does indicate that the near surface inversion\nmay linger and continue to promote low stratus through much of \nthe afternoon and evening. Low temperatures this morning range \nfrom the lower teens where cloud cover is lacking (e.g. the \nwest), up into the mid 20s in the south central. With clearing \nskies through the afternoon and a transient upper level sliding \nacross the northern Plains, highs this afternoon are forecast to\nnudge into the lower to mid 40s across the west, while \nlingering clouds across central North Dakota will keep highs a \nlittle cooler in the mid to upper 40s.\n\nAn influx of mid level moisture behind the aforementioned \ntransient ridge is expected to allow for a band of mid level \nclouds to slide across North Dakota overnight, before skies \nclear from northwest to southeast through the late morning and \nafternoon. Persistant southerly surface winds and the near zonal\nupper level flow aloft are expected to push high temperatures \ninto the 40s and even the lower 50s for Thanksgiving day. \n\nAs we look toward Friday and the weekend, a shift in weather \nconditions over the northern Plains as a northern stream wave / \nclosed low is progged to slide along the International Border \nearly Friday through Saturday. The main concern related to this \nwill be the potential for breezy to strong westerly winds \nthrough the day Friday as a surface low and associated cold \nfront is lifted across the area. Increasing CAA and steepening \nlow level lapse rates are expected to help mix down stronger \nwinds from a develop H8 jet sliding in across the west through \nthe day, though the top end of the winds may be limited by the \nstrongest CAA preceding the core of the jet by a few hours. We \ncan at least strong westerly winds approaching wind advisory \ncriteria late Friday morning through Friday afternoon, with \ngusts approaching 40 MPH. Otherwise, low chances for light \nprecipitation are also possible, mainly along the cold front as \nit lifts up across the far southwest. With the displacement of a\nmuch cooler airmass over the northern Plains behind this front,\nmore seasonable conditions are expected this weekend and into \nearly next week. With a transition to northwesterly flow during \nthis timeframe, low chances for precpitation can be anticipated \njust about any day given the potential for shortwave \ndisturbances traversing this upper level pattern though no \nstrong signal currently exists for meaningful precpitation at \nthis time.",
